INT. MINIVAN - AUTOMATIC CAR WASH - DAY

Soapy foam slides down the windshield in thick white ropes. Giant blue brushes thump and whirl. The minivan sits motionless on the track, trapped in a dim, wet cave.

CAROL DREXLER (40s, bun, sunglasses on her head, a coffee cup that has gone cold) grips the wheel with both hands. In the passenger seat, her daughter MAYA DREXLER (15, hoodie, knees up, phone dark in her lap) stares at the side mirror.

Neither speaks. The brushes thump.

CAROL
Seatbelt.

MAYA
It's on.

CAROL
It's behind you.

Maya yanks it across. Clicks it in. Hard.

CAROL (CONT'D)
Thank you.

MAYA
You're welcome.

The brushes sweep over the roof with a long howl. Carol flinches. Maya notices and pretends not to.

CAROL
(to herself)
Twelve dollars. For this.

MAYA
You could have just washed it at home.

CAROL
We don't have a hose.

MAYA
We have a hose. Dad has a hose.

Silence. Carol's knuckles whiten.

CAROL
I'm not talking about your father.

MAYA
You just did.

CAROL
You brought him up.

MAYA
I said "hose."

CAROL
You said it like a weapon.

Maya turns, finally, to look at her mother. Her eyes are red-rimmed but dry.

MAYA
Can we just go?

CAROL
Where? We're in a tunnel, Maya.

MAYA
Then can we stop pretending this is about the car?

Carol laughs once, sharp. It cracks. She lifts the cup, drinks the cold coffee, grimaces.

CAROL
(to the windshield)
Everyone out there gets a clean car. I get this.

MAYA
Mom. Out loud, please.

CAROL
I said it out loud.

MAYA
You said it to the foam.

Carol sets the cup in the holder too hard. Coffee sloshes over her wrist. She ignores it.

CAROL
You want out loud? Fine. I'm tired.

MAYA
Okay.

CAROL
I'm tired of driving you to your father's. I'm tired of driving you back. I'm tired of him calling you before I do.

MAYA
He doesn't--

CAROL
He does.

MAYA
He calls to ask about me, not about you.

CAROL
Is that supposed to make it better?

Maya says nothing. The brushes slam against the van. Water roars. The cabin shakes.

MAYA
(quietly)
He asked if you were okay.

Carol freezes. Her hand drifts to her mouth.

CAROL
When?

MAYA
Sunday.

CAROL
What did you say?

MAYA
I said you were fine.

CAROL
Well, that was a lie.

MAYA
I know.

A long beat. The water slows. The foam slides off the glass in thick sheets.

Carol reaches across the console. Maya doesn't pull away. Their hands meet on the cup holder, sticky with coffee.

CAROL
I'm sorry I yelled at the car wash.

MAYA
You didn't yell.

CAROL
I thought about it.

Maya almost smiles. Wipes her nose on her sleeve.

MAYA
Can I say something?

CAROL
Always.

MAYA
I don't want to pick.

CAROL
(softly)
I know, baby.

MAYA
I mean it. I don't want to ride in one car and then the other like I'm a package.

CAROL
You're not a package.

MAYA
It feels like it.

Carol closes her eyes. Maya squeezes her hand. They sit, joined, as the dryers roar and warm air blasts across the windshield.

The light ahead turns green. A gate lifts. Daylight spills in.

Carol opens her eyes. Both women look at the bright rectangle of the exit. Neither moves to start the engine.

CAROL (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Let's sit a minute.

MAYA
Okay.

The dryers sigh and fall quiet. The van rests in the doorway of the sun.
